Folding it in would BREAK a green test. tests/unit/core_AgeingKernel_test.cpp:577-578:
    REQUIRE_THROWS_AS(core::SpmTransportCache(std::numeric_limits<int>::max()), std::length_error);
That passes only because of the INT_MAX/2 guard. A generic helper testing `lanes > SIZE_MAX/per_lane` with per_lane=2 compares INT_MAX (~2.15e9) against SIZE_MAX/2 (~9.22e18) on 64-bit, does not throw, and the REQUIRE_THROWS_AS fails. So SpmTransportCache is a fourth site only in silhouette. The helper genuinely serves two sites: SpmObservableScratch and AgeingScratchStorage (AgeingKernel.hpp:67-82), which are structurally identical modulo the message literal — a `const char *what` parameter covers that, and it is not a flag/branch. Tests use REQUIRE_THROWS_AS (type only, no Matches()), so message text is unconstrained.

PROPOSAL DEFECT. `assert(count <= storage_.size() - cursor)` underflows when cursor > size() — exactly the corrupt state it is meant to catch — making the RHS enormous and the assert vacuously true. Use `cursor + count <= storage_.size()`.

HONEST LIMIT the reviewer overstates: naming the constants does not structurally prevent drift — a new field still needs a manual bump of shared_lane_fields. The equality assert is what catches it, and only in Debug. That is adequate in practice: 15 test sites construct SpmObservableScratch (core_AgeingKernel/LithiumPlating/Lam/Sei/SpmElectrical/SpmStress/SurfaceCrack/ThermalLumped) and CLAUDE.md runs ctest in Debug.

DIGIT-IDENTICAL: yes. 2*6+9 = 21 exactly, so per_lane, the allocation size, and every subspan offset are unchanged. No floating-point expression is touched — no reassociation, no loop-to-reduce, no FMA fusion. Asserts are non-mutating and NDEBUG-stripped. Deleting 420-421 changes nothing reachable: checked_lane_count already threw the same type and same message, so core_AgeingKernel_test.cpp:573-574 stay green.

