SpmObservables.hpp:463-468:
      const Real cs = output.concentration[d][lane];
      const Real z_surface = spm_scalar::surfaceStoichiometry(
        cs, electrode.cs_max);
      if (!(primal_value(z_surface) > 0.0 && primal_value(z_surface) < 1.0))
        return slide::Status::Invalid_states;

src/core/Numeric.hpp:24-37 states the hazard and supplies the remedy:
 * IEEE-754 finite check that clang cannot discard under `-ffast-math`/`-Ofast`.
 * `std::isfinite` is not a validation primitive in those builds because the compiler is
 * permitted to assume NaN and infinity never occur and fold the check to true.
  ...
  volatile std::uint64_t bits = std::bit_cast<std::uint64_t>(value);
  return (bits & exponent_mask) != exponent_mask;

The build really is finite-math: cmake/StandardProjectSettings.cmake:58-59 and 64-65 add `-Ofast` and `-ffast-math` to every Release C++ TU, and cmake/SlideCoreTarget.cmake:49-51 restores strict FP for only two files -- ForwardSensitivity.cpp and PackSolverValidation.cpp. SpmBatch.cpp, which instantiates SpmPipeline and hence computeSpmObservables, is NOT in that list.

Under IEEE the code is correct (NaN makes both comparisons false, so !(false) returns Invalid_states); under -ffinite-math-only the compiler may rewrite `!(x > 0 && x < 1)` as `x <= 0 || x >= 1`, which is false for NaN -- the invalid state is then accepted and a NaN voltage propagates into the terminal-voltage row. The sibling kernel already does this correctly: src/core/SpmStress.hpp:162-163 uses
      if (!is_finite_primal(maximum))
        return slide::Status::Numerical_failure;

proposal:
Make the gate fast-math-proof and keep the range test:

      if (!is_finite_primal(z_surface)
          || !(primal_value(z_surface) > 0.0 && primal_value(z_surface) < 1.0))
        return slide::Status::Invalid_states;

`is_finite_primal` (Numeric.hpp:85-97) already dispatches correctly for Real = double and Real = Dual, and SpmObservables.hpp already includes the header transitively via CellDesign/StateArena. Behaviour under strict IEEE is unchanged (a NaN was already rejected); the change only removes the compiler's licence to delete the rejection in Release.

Registered test to add alongside (tests/unit/core_SpmObservables_test.cpp), and this is the pass/fail band: build one lane whose z-rows produce cs <= 0 and a second whose cs >= cs_max, REQUIRE computeSpmObservables == Status::Invalid_states in BOTH Debug and Release; then a third lane seeded so `cs` is NaN (set the arena diffusion_coefficient row to 0.0 so Dt == 0 and the feedthrough term is inf-inf) and REQUIRE Invalid_states there too. The third assertion is the one that fails today under -Ofast.

