Why exact equality is legitimate here, and why I am NOT proposing the more obvious test: T - T is exactly 0.0 in IEEE-754 for any finite T, k * 0.0 is exactly 0.0 for finite k > 0, and 0.0 + (-0.0) == 0.0, so the result is bit-exact independent of accumulation order, and `== 0.0` compares true for both signed zeros. The tempting alternative -- assert sum(q_ext) + sum(boundary_heat) == 0.0 on a general multi-edge graph -- is NOT a valid exact oracle: each endpoint total is summed in incident order and the endpoint totals are then summed again, so the cancellation is only exact when the magnitudes happen to be exactly representable (as they are in the existing 2-edge case at line 98, which is why that one passes). The isothermal limit is the invariant that holds for every graph.

