verdict.revised_proposal:
Extract a private helper and call it from both sites:

```cpp
// segment_[bin] <= x_.size() - 2 for every bin: build()'s advance loop is guarded by
// `segment_index + 1 < x.size() - 1`, so it can only leave segment_index at x.size() - 2.
// That is what makes the unguarded x_[i + 1] read below legal (i + 1 <= x_.size() - 1).
[[nodiscard]] std::size_t segment_of(real_t x) const noexcept
{
  const auto raw_bin = static_cast<std::size_t>((x - x_.front()) * inv_bin_width_);
  const auto bin = std::min(raw_bin, segment_.size() - 1);
  std::size_t i = segment_[bin];
  if (i + 1 < x_.size() - 1 && x >= x_[i + 1])
    ++i;
  else if (x < x_[i] && i > 0)
    --i;
  return i;
}
```

Two corrections to the original proposal:

1. The comment must record `segment_[bin] <= x_.size() - 2`, NOT `x.size() - 3`. The reviewer's cap is provably wrong (trace above: a 3-knot curve stores index 1 == x.size()-2, while x.size()-3 == 0). Since the whole stated point of the extraction is to stop the reader re-deriving this proof per copy, shipping the wrong number would be worse than the duplication.

2. Keep derivative()'s operand order (`i + 1 < x_.size() - 1 && x >= x_[i + 1]`) as the canonical body, not eval()'s. Both are safe, but bounds-check-before-read is the order a future reader can validate locally without needing the build() invariant at all — it degrades safely if anyone ever weakens build().

Then: `eval` becomes `const std::size_t i = segment_of(xp);` and `derivative` becomes `const std::size_t i = segment_of(x);`, deleting 6 lines.

