verdict.reason:
I tried to refute this and could not. All three factual legs hold.

(1) The quoted code exists verbatim. CudaSpmRuntime.cu:119-125 is exactly:
    if (!(surface_stoichiometry[domain] > 0.0
          && surface_stoichiometry[domain] < 1.0)
        || !isfinite(surface_stoichiometry[domain])) {
      atomicExch(status, 1);
      terminal_voltage[lane] = nan("");
      return;
    }
and :182-186 is exactly:
  if (!isfinite(voltage)) {
    atomicExch(status, 1);
    terminal_voltage[lane] = nan("");
    return;
  }
The two failure arms are byte-identical (three statements, same order, same operands modulo indentation). No numerically meaningful difference between them, so the "duplicate" is genuine.

(2) The unreachability claim survives exhaustive IEEE-754 case analysis on x = surface_stoichiometry[domain]. x=NaN: `NaN > 0.0` is false, `&&` short-circuits false, `!false`=true -> first disjunct true, `||` short-circuits and `!isfinite` is never even evaluated. x=+inf: `inf>0` true, `inf<1` false -> `!(false)`=true. x=-inf: `-inf>0` false -> true. x finite outside (0,1): true. x finite inside (0,1): first disjunct false, and `!isfinite(x)`=false. So for EVERY double the whole expression equals `!(x > 0.0 && x < 1.0)` alone. sNaN behaves identically (comparisons return false). The disjunct is never the deciding term.

(3) I checked the fast-math escape hatch, which is the one thing that could have rescued the disjunct, and it closes the case rather than opening it. cmake/StandardProjectSettings.cmake:57-59 scopes fast-math to `$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>` only ("$<$<AND:$<CONFIG:Release>,$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>>:-ffast-math>"), so the CUDA lane compiles under nvcc default IEEE semantics and the case analysis above is the operative one. And under fast-math the disjunct would be worthless anyway by the project's own doctrine at Numeric.hpp:24-28: "`std::isfinite` is not a validation primitive in those builds because the compiler is permitted to assume NaN and infinity never occur and fold the check to true." So the term is redundant under IEEE and useless under fast-math. Both roads lead to deletion.

(4) Comparison sites: ForwardSensitivity.cpp:97 is verbatim `if (!(stoichiometry[d].value > 0.0 && stoichiometry[d].value < 1.0))`. The SpmObservables quote is verbatim but the LINE NUMBER IS WRONG — it is line 466, not 665; the file is only 547 lines (`wc -l` = 547), so :665 does not exist. Content correct, citation wrong. A ripgrep for `> 0\.0 && .*< 1\.0` across src/ returns exactly three hits (ForwardSensitivity.cpp:97, SpmFactory.cpp:156, SpmObservables.hpp:466), none of which pairs the range test with isfinite — so "every other backend uses the range test alone" is confirmed.

(5) Digit-identical: yes, provable by construction. Deleting the disjunct changes control flow for no input (leg 2), so every store is unchanged. The helper contains zero floating-point arithmetic — one atomicExch on an int and one nan("") store, same order — so there is no reassociation, no fused multiply-add, no summation-order change. Note the honest limit: this is case analysis, not a recorded run; I did not build (PLAN's ledger records the CUDA lane needs MSVC cl.exe on PATH). The claim must be discharged against the pinned gate, which the ledger fixes at "CudaSpmBatch unchanged at 433,671/4".

(6) No contract violated. PC-10 is not engaged — the guard is a validity range test, not a physics formula, and the change makes the three backends converge rather than duplicate. No hot-path allocation (PC-1), no exception/lock/IO (PC-4), and the helper is a direct call to an anonymous-namespace `__device__` function in the same TU, not a function pointer or virtual dispatch (PC-2/PC-5). It stays inside a .cu anonymous namespace so no api/support header gains an internal include (MC-5, D-42). MC-1 line count drops.

(7) The secondary doc observation is also correct and correctly scoped as doc-only. At the :125 early return, z has been advanced for BOTH domains (the domain loop 74-99 wraps the mode loop 90-98 and writes z in place through `at(...)`) while elapsed_time/charge_throughput/energy_throughput at :190-193 have not. CudaSpmBatch.cpp:311 is precisely `slide::Status CudaSpmBatch::restore()`. CudaSpmBatch.hpp:46 is precisely `/** Queue one accepted exact-modal step. This call allocates and synchronizes nothing. */` and indeed says nothing about the half-advanced arena on a rejected step.

